<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brough, East Riding of Yorkshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brough-on-Humber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brough is a town on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> estuary in the East Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It stands on the site of the Roman fort and town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petuaria">Petuaria</a>, which an inscription names as a vicus of the Parisi with its own aedile, and which probably served as their civitas capital. The settlement guarded a ferry crossing on the Roman road from Lincoln to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brough-east-riding-of-yorkshire.jpg" alt="Brough, East Riding of Yorkshire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Harrop · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brough_sunflower_sculpture_-_geograph.org.uk_-_8304322.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the place was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petuaria">Petuaria</a>. It marked the southern end of the Roman road now called Cade's Road, which ran roughly north for a hundred miles to Pons Aelius, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a>. The present name comes from the Old English burh, a fortification, a word related to borough and burgh.</p><p>In 1239 the Archbishop of York made Brough a town with the same liberties as Beverley, but there is no record that these liberties were ever used, and for centuries the settlement remained a village. The highwayman Dick Turpin lodged at the Ferry Inn at Brough from about June 1737 under the alias John Palmer, or Parmen, and lived in and travelled to and from Brough until he was captured and executed for horse theft in 1739.</p><p>Brough has a long association with aircraft making. BAE Systems, formerly British Aerospace, built the Hawk Advanced Jet Trainer at Brough Aerodrome, and new aircraft flew from its runway to Warton near Preston for final testing and painting. The company announced 450 job losses at Brough on 3 April 2008 and, on 1 March 2012, the end of manufacturing there with 845 redundancies. Manufacturing at the plant ceased on Christmas Eve 2020, by which time building had begun on the runway as part of the Brough South housing development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Brough, pronounced Bruf locally, lies on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a>, and the Yorkshire Wolds Way National Trail passes its eastern end. With the neighbouring village of Elloughton it forms the civil parish of Elloughton-cum-Brough, and at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the town had 13,331 inhabitants. Better rail links and new housing have changed its social make-up and raised local incomes.</p><p>The town lies about a mile south of the A63 road from Hull to the M62. Brough railway station, on the line from Hull to Selby and Doncaster, has direct trains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and the former air traffic control building of the aerodrome is now the Brough Business Centre. The town's primary schools are Brough Primary and Hunsley Primary, and the nearest secondary school is South Hunsley School in Melton.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brough, East Riding of Yorkshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ptolemy later listed Petuaria as their town, and an inscription from <b>Brough-on-Humber</b> names an aedile of the vicus of Petuaria; the Parisi probably formed a small civitas of Roman Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii-britain">Parisii (Britain)</a></li></ul></section>
